1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,448
Imagine you spent ten years becoming a doctor. You passed every exam. You treated thousands of patients. Then you move to a new country, and the first job you can get is driving a taxi.

2
00:00:15,148 --> 00:00:27,052
This happens to engineers, nurses, teachers, accountants, pharmacists. Skilled people who cross a border and find that their qualification does not cross with them.

3
00:00:27,752 --> 00:00:40,880
Now imagine a different person. She graduated last year. She has no career yet. She moves to the same country, starts from the bottom, and calls it the beginning.

4
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:54,156
Same skills. Same country. Two very different stories. Which one is harder on the mind? That is the question we set out to answer.

5
00:00:54,856 --> 00:01:10,960
We are PYROS, a research channel. We wanted to know whether experienced professionals who migrate and must start over feel more low, more hopeless, more worn down than recent graduates who migrate before their career has really begun.

6
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:26,900
Our idea was simple. If you have already built a career, an identity and an income, losing them hurts. If you never had them, there is nothing to lose yet, and the move can feel like a start rather than an interruption.

7
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:42,912
So we did what researchers do. We searched the medical literature, read every study that fitted, pulled out the numbers, and let the evidence speak. Our full method, data and code are public, and the link is in the description.

8
00:01:43,612 --> 00:01:56,236
One honest warning before we begin. This is a rapid look, using one medical database, not a full systematic review. What we say today is what that evidence shows.

9
00:01:56,936 --> 00:02:08,960
We started with two hundred and fifteen research papers. After reading titles, summaries and full texts, seven studies fitted our question closely enough to use.

10
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:22,740
And here is the first surprise. Not one of the seven compared the two groups we cared about. Nobody had put experienced professionals next to recent graduates and measured who felt worse.

11
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:31,720
That is a finding in itself. This question, which affects millions of families, has not been directly studied.

12
00:02:32,420 --> 00:02:39,764
But the seven studies still tell us a great deal about when the mind suffers after a skilled move, and when it does not.

13
00:02:40,464 --> 00:02:46,248
Before we look at them, three things to keep in mind, because we will be honest about the limits.

14
00:02:46,948 --> 00:03:00,484
First, most of these studies took a single snapshot. They asked people once. A snapshot can show that two things go together. It cannot prove that one caused the other.

15
00:03:01,184 --> 00:03:11,624
Second, some studies were small. One had twenty-one people. When a study is small, we say "promising", not "proven".

16
00:03:12,324 --> 00:03:23,004
Third, the studies used different questionnaires and different countries, so we never add their numbers together. We lay them side by side and look for a pattern.

17
00:03:23,704 --> 00:03:40,912
Let us start with the good news. In Germany, researchers compared nurses who had migrated with nurses born in the country, all doing the same home-care work. They asked about low mood, poor sleep, loss of interest, the signs of depressive symptoms.

18
00:03:41,612 --> 00:03:51,932
The migrant nurses were no worse off than their local colleagues. About one in five in each group had troubling symptoms. The same number.

19
00:03:52,632 --> 00:04:01,488
In Finland, another study asked the same kind of question of foreign-born and Finnish nurses. Again, no difference.

20
00:04:02,188 --> 00:04:17,764
In Toronto, Canada, researchers looked at Korean immigrants in four kinds of work: professionals, small business owners, office workers and manual workers. The professionals had the lowest levels of depressive symptoms of all four.

21
00:04:18,464 --> 00:04:29,096
Notice what these three studies have in common. The people were already working in their field, or at least working. They had made it through the door.

22
00:04:29,796 --> 00:04:39,612
The message is not that migration is painless. The message is that once a skilled migrant is practising again, they look much like everyone else.

23
00:04:40,312 --> 00:04:48,328
So where does the pain live? The studies point to one place: the gap between arriving and working at your level.

24
00:04:49,028 --> 00:05:03,668
In the United States, researchers asked more than three thousand immigrants from Latin America and Asia a simple question. Picture a ladder of society. Where did you stand at home? Where do you stand now?

25
00:05:04,368 --> 00:05:19,992
People who had dropped three or more rungs were three times as likely to have had a serious episode of depression in the past year, compared with people whose position had not changed. That held true whatever their education, income or years in the country.

26
00:05:20,692 --> 00:05:42,100
A more recent study followed people forced to leave Ukraine and Russia after the war began. Almost all were university educated. Among the Ukrainian women in Poland, only about one in four were working in a job that matched their qualification. Among the Russians, mostly in Serbia, about seven in ten were.

27
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:59,672
The group with far more people working below their level also had far more people with depressive symptoms. More than half in one group, under four in ten in the other. And in the Russian group, working below your qualification was itself linked to worse symptoms.

28
00:06:00,372 --> 00:06:09,036
These two groups differed in many ways, so this is a clue, not a proof. But it points the same direction as the ladder.

29
00:06:09,736 --> 00:06:32,752
And in one hospital sample in Turkey, among five hundred and seventy Syrian doctors who had fled the war, about one in ten had moderate or severe depressive symptoms. Those who were planning to go back, and those without experience in primary care, were doing worse. Those who still saw themselves as doctors first, not as refugees, were doing better.

30
00:06:33,452 --> 00:06:38,852
Numbers cannot say everything. Two studies simply listened.

31
00:06:39,552 --> 00:07:00,480
In Germany, twenty Syrian doctors described applying for their medical licence. Rules that changed from month to month. Sixteen different states with sixteen different lists of documents. Phone calls that were never answered. One doctor said he had called the office more than five hundred times.

32
00:07:01,180 --> 00:07:11,836
They used the words depressed, irritated, in despair. One surgeon, who had operated on war injuries, was refused even an unpaid placement.

33
00:07:12,536 --> 00:07:25,448
The three women in that study told a further story. In job interviews they were asked about their headscarf, not their training. One said: a piece of cloth determines my destination.

34
00:07:26,148 --> 00:07:40,068
Yet the same study heard something else too. Most of the doctors said that once they reached an actual job interview, they were treated with respect, as a colleague. The wall was the paperwork, not the profession.

35
00:07:40,768 --> 00:07:59,032
In the United States, twenty-three highly educated Iranians, most of them doctoral students, described something else: visas that allowed only one entry, so that leaving to see a sick parent could mean never coming back. Some had not seen their families for six, seven, eight years.

36
00:07:59,732 --> 00:08:06,212
They spoke of insomnia, panic and depression, of putting life on hold because they could not plan.

37
00:08:06,912 --> 00:08:14,808
These are not studies that measured symptoms with a questionnaire. But they show us what the waiting feels like from inside.

38
00:08:15,508 --> 00:08:23,332
So what helps? The studies give us five answers, and we will be honest about how strong each one is.

39
00:08:24,032 --> 00:08:44,312
One. Getting back into your field, even before the licence arrives. In Norway, doctors, pharmacists, a dentist, a nurse and a radiographer who were still waiting for accreditation spent six months as assistant teachers in university health courses, each with a mentor from their own profession.

40
00:08:45,012 --> 00:09:01,116
Their general mental health and their sense of well-being improved. Their specific depressive symptoms did not change, and the study was small, so we call this promising, not proven. But listen to what they said: "I felt like myself again."

41
00:09:01,816 --> 00:09:20,248
Something else happened in those families. Mothers said their children began to speak of them differently. "My mother works at the university." A door pass, a reason to leave the house, a walk with colleagues at lunch. Small things that rebuild a person.

42
00:09:20,948 --> 00:09:36,884
Two. Good colleagues and a secure job. Among migrant nurses in Germany, good relationships with co-workers and managers protected against distress. Short-term contracts and having no say at work did the opposite.

43
00:09:37,584 --> 00:09:46,872
Three. Holding on to who you are. The Syrian doctors in Turkey who kept their identity as physicians were doing better.

44
00:09:47,572 --> 00:10:02,188
Four. Language. In Toronto and in Poland, language difficulties went with more symptoms. Every hour spent learning the local language is an investment in your mind, not only your career.

45
00:10:02,888 --> 00:10:20,936
Five. Welcome. Among the Ukrainian women in Poland, the single strongest predictor of depressive symptoms was how the local population treated them. Not their education. Not their housing. Whether they felt wanted.

46
00:10:21,636 --> 00:10:43,596
And one more, about how we cope. In both the Ukrainian and Russian groups, people who said "I give up, I do not know what to do" had more symptoms. People who said "I get organised and do my best" had fewer. That is one study, and coping is easier to advise than to do. But it is worth knowing.

47
00:10:44,296 --> 00:10:51,232
Now back to our question. Does the experienced professional suffer more than the recent graduate?

48
00:10:51,932 --> 00:11:07,868
Honestly: nobody has measured it. What the evidence does suggest is narrower. The danger is not being experienced. The danger is the time spent not working at your level. And it eases once you are.

49
00:11:08,568 --> 00:11:20,112
Whether a recent graduate passes through that time more easily is a fair guess. It is not yet a finding. If researchers test it, we will update this video.

50
00:11:20,812 --> 00:11:56,020
I trained as a doctor in Nigeria. I moved to Germany to study global health, and I know the waiting room in this video. It is not a place. It is a feeling: qualified, ready, and not allowed to start.  What I wish someone had told me is this. The paperwork is slow, but it is not a judgement on you. Stay near your profession in any way you can. Learn the language like it is a rotation. And find one person who calls you colleague. That is what carried me.

51
00:11:56,720 --> 00:12:08,840
If you are a skilled migrant in the waiting room right now: the studies say get close to your profession in any way you can, learn the language, keep your name for yourself, and find people who treat you as a colleague.

52
00:12:09,540 --> 00:12:20,916
If you employ, license or live next to skilled migrants: answer the phone, shorten the wait, give real contracts, and make people feel wanted. Welcome is medicine.

53
00:12:21,616 --> 00:12:30,904
Our full report, data and code are free at the link below. If this helped you, share it with one person who is starting over.
